&lt;p class=MsoNormal align=center style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:center;
line-height:18.0pt'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>Including the Body in the
Image&lt;/span>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
.5in;line-height:18.0pt'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span>&lt;/p>
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
.5in;line-height:18.0pt'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>In this chapter we
address more deliberately the issue noted before. A long-standing debate about
the image concerns whether to include the body in it. There are three ways to
relate the image and the body. The body might be (a) &lt;u>part of&lt;/u> the image,
(b) &lt;u>involved with&lt;/u> the image, or (c)&lt;u> analogous to&lt;/u> the image. Several
considerations go into deciding whether the body is&lt;i> &lt;/i>&lt;u>in&lt;/u>, &lt;u>with&lt;/u>,
or &lt;u>like&lt;/u> the image.&lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>IMAGE AND LIKENESS</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/nature-of-man-book/image-and-likeness/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/nature-of-man-book/image-and-likeness/</guid><description>&lt;style>
