	{font-family:"Tms Rmn",serif;}
 /* Page Definitions */
&lt;/style>
&lt;div class="word-content">
&lt;p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:-.5in;text-align:justify;text-indent:
.5in;line-height:18.0pt'>&lt;span style='font-size:12.0pt'>We have not made a
distinction between “image” and “likeness,” even though Genesis &lt;b>1&lt;/b>:26
uses two words to compare mankind and God. &lt;b>(a)&lt;/b> Nothing in the context
distinguishes them. Furthermore, &lt;b>(b) &lt;/b>in most other passages only one
term occurs, &lt;i>image&lt;/i> in Genesis &lt;b>9&lt;/b>:6-7; 1 Corinthians &lt;b>11&lt;/b>:7;
James &lt;b>3&lt;/b>:9 and &lt;i>likeness&lt;/i> in Genesis &lt;b>5&lt;/b>:2 in an otherwise
word-for-word parallel to &lt;b>1&lt;/b>:26. &lt;i>Like&lt;/i> in Genesis &lt;b>3&lt;/b>:5, 22
translates a different item than in &lt;b>1&lt;/b>:26.&lt;span style='position:relative;
top:-3.0pt'>1&lt;/span>           &lt;/span>&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>THE IMAGE AND ETHICS</title><link>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/nature-of-man-book/the-image-and-ethics/</link><pubDate>Thu, 02 Apr 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://christir.org/essays/topics/christian-doctrine/mankind-anthropology/nature-of-man-book/the-image-and-ethics/</guid><description>&lt;style>
